&lt;p&gt;This&amp;nbsp;mini-symposium&amp;nbsp;will bring together investigators and clinicians from across Columbia to introduce the initiative, share highlights of ongoing research in hematologic malignancies, and outline opportunities for collaboration, education, and future programming.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The event will feature an overview of the initiative&amp;rsquo;s goals and structure, research presentations spanning discovery through clinical application, and time for discussion and connection across disciplines.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All faculty, trainees, and staff with an interest in hematologic malignancies are encouraged to attend.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Save your seat below.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many stalking behaviors are normalized in movies, TV shows, and social media. This interactive activity breaks down what we see on screen and how it can show up in real life.&amp;nbsp;Swing by Lerner Ramps during Stalking Awareness Month (January) to spin the wheel, watch a short media clip, and decide: &lt;strong&gt;healthy behavior or stalking?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Grab some swag and treats while you&amp;rsquo;re there and stick around to chat with Sexual Violence Response (SVR) staff about resources, prevention, and ways to look out for yourself and others.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Date: &lt;/strong&gt;Tuesday, January 27, 2026, from 12:30-2:00PM &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;The Interchurch Center, 61 Claremont Avenue (Entrance between 119th &amp;amp; 120th Street)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Series:&lt;/strong&gt; Devotion and Distraction&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker:&lt;/strong&gt; Lital Levy (Princeton University)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Respondent:&lt;/strong&gt; Seth Kimmel (Columbia University)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a simple story that we now tell ourselves about secularization, the European Enlightenment plays a key role: It&amp;rsquo;s the period when scientific inquiry and rational thought became ascendent, while religious practice receded to a more limited private sphere, a shift that coincided with new paradigms of toleration and liberty. The truth is of course more complicated, as theologians from various religious communities reimagined both religious practice and belief in dialogue with the philosophical and political trends of the moment. Along with their neighbors from other religious communities, how did participants in the Jewish Enlightenment, or Haskalah, come to view the new distractions, technological transformations, and dream of universal knowledge that characterized modern life before the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Free and open to the public &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration is required&amp;mdash;&lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdaaDEcYwMgicdSVKlaPmkk-_4lSW8rws30ad_QrccDqWTWuQ/viewform" target="_blank"&gt;Please register here in advance to reserve your spot&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All prospective attendees must register by 11:59PM on Monday, January 26. Registration will close at that time.&lt;/p&gt;

