&lt;h2&gt;Please join us on &lt;strong&gt;Monday, February 2, at 12:00 p.m. ET&lt;/strong&gt; on &lt;strong&gt;Zoom&lt;/strong&gt; for the 2026 Naomi Prawer Kadar Memorial Lecture, &amp;ldquo;Snow and Herring: The Work of Modern Yiddish Culture,&amp;rdquo; featuring &lt;strong&gt;Dr. Jordan Finkin.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In its millennium-long history, Yiddish has appeared in many guises, which have only proliferated in the modern world. With its fabric torn in the middle of the twentieth century, the field of Yiddish today confronts the perturbations of rupture and renewal. The stuff of Yiddish today is culture work. What does that work look like? How do contemporary practitioners navigate the tensions between the work of cultural preservation and creative reinvention? What visions for the future emerge from such efforts?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dr. Jordan Finkin&lt;/strong&gt; is the Library Director and Rare Book and Manuscript Librarian at the Hebrew Union College in Cincinnati. A scholar of modern Yiddish literature, Dr. Finkin is the author of several scholarly monographs as well as numerous academic essays and articles. A productive literary translator from Yiddish, German, and French, Dr. Finkin is also the founder and director of Naydus Press, a non-profit publisher of Yiddish literature in English translation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/the-2026-naomi-prawer-kadar-memorial-lecture-with-jordan-finkin-tickets-1977312448762?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;Click here to register for this event&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Supported by the generosity of the Naomi Foundation.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Naomi Prawer Kadar Annual Memorial Lecture&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; provides an opportunity for the public to explore topics of Yiddish language and linguistics, the history of Yiddish, Yiddish children&amp;rsquo;s literature and education. The lecture is supported by the &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Naomi Prawer Kadar Foundation, Inc.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;, which is dedicated to reimagining education. The Naomi Foundation champions Yiddish, Naomi&amp;rsquo;s lifelong passion, as a vibrant, rich, and contemporary language. The Naomi Foundation advances the teaching and learning of Yiddish, particularly in academic and scholarly settings.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Please join the Herbert Lehman Center for American History and the Institute for Research in African-American Studies for a discussion with Brian Kwoba (University of Memphis) of his&amp;nbsp;biography&amp;nbsp;of Hubert H. Harrison, a long-forgotten founder of Black radical thought. The conversation will be moderated by Brent Hayes Edwards.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The significance of Hubert Henry Harrison (1883&amp;ndash;1927)&amp;mdash;as a journalist, activist, and educator&amp;mdash;lies in his innovation of radical solutions to radical injustices.&amp;nbsp;He did far more than cultivate the rich, dark soil in which the so-called &amp;ldquo;Harlem Renaissance&amp;rdquo; would take root. Harrison also played a pivotal role in the rise of Marcus Garvey and the emergence of the largest international organization of African people in modern history. Because of his fearless radicalism, however, he has been erased from popular memory. Brian Kwoba&amp;#39;s biography draws deeply on his research in&amp;nbsp;the &lt;a href="https://dlc.library.columbia.edu/hubert_h_harrison" target="_blank"&gt;Hubert Harrison Papers&lt;/a&gt;, which are held at the Rare Book &amp;amp; Manuscript Library.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;Brian Kwoba has written a beautiful, intellectual biography as radical and original as its subject. He excavates Hubert H. Harrison&amp;mdash;brilliant Marxist, Black nationalist, internationalist, and gender rebel&amp;mdash;revealing dimensions even his most scrupulous chroniclers missed.&amp;rdquo;&amp;mdash;Robin D. G. Kelley, author of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Freedom Dreams: The Black Radical Imagination&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on February 2, 2026 to attend this event in-person.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Ukrainian Studies Program at the Harriman Institute for a book talk by &lt;strong&gt;Sophia Wilson&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-andryczyk/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Andryczyk&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sophia Wilson will discuss her book, &amp;quot;Maidan: Ukraine&amp;#39;s Democratic Revolution&amp;quot; - a carefully researched account of the underbelly of the Ukrainian revolutionary process in the winter of 2013-14. The book investigates how participants self-organized to create the resistance, why the peaceful movement eventually turned to violence, and how the revolutionary process changed those who came to change the country. The Ukrainian state used violence and violations of due process to suppress the resistance, thereby declaring new boundaries in rights relations. Wilson&amp;#39;s book shows how the people pushed back in multiple arenas &amp;ndash; the protest square, courtrooms, hospitals, churches, and media &amp;ndash; to successfully challenge the constitutionality of the state&amp;rsquo;s actions.&lt;br /&gt;
(https://www.mqup.ca/Books/M/Maidan)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join ERM program Part-Time Lecturer Melody Feinberg as she interviews Ron Cathcart, Vice Chair of the Natixis US Risk Committee and former U.S. representative to the Policy Development Group of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision. Cathcart previously served as head of Enterprise Risk Supervision at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and chief enterprise risk officer at Washington Mutual, with additional senior risk roles at CIBC, Bank One, and RBC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ron will discuss how boards can elevate oversight by embracing strategic risk dialogue, sharpening information flow, and embedding scenario analysis into decision-making. He will emphasize the importance of board composition, management alignment, and a forward-looking mindset that treats risk as a strategic capability rather than a compliance exercise. Boards must be willing to challenge management and engage deeply in discussions around risk appetite and strategic choices.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cathcart will also explore the rising importance of scenario-based planning to anticipate disruption and the need for management to bring data-driven &amp;ldquo;what-if&amp;rdquo; analyses to the boardroom. Ron will highlight emerging risks reshaping oversight&amp;mdash;including AI, cyber threats, and geopolitical shifts&amp;mdash;and illustrate how a proactive, forward-leaning board can help shape the organization&amp;rsquo;s risk posture, resilience, and long-term success.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
∙&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Ron Cathcart&lt;/strong&gt;, Vice Chair of the Natixis US Risk Committee and former U.S. representative to the Policy Development Group of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision&lt;br /&gt;
∙&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Melody Feinberg&lt;/strong&gt;, Independent Board Member, Axiom Bank and Security Mutual Life Insurance Company of New York; former Chief Risk Officer of Federal Home Loan Bank of New York&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
