&lt;p&gt;This fireside chat will feature &lt;strong&gt;Henry Ginsberg, MD&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;strong&gt;Henry Colecraft, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;, two accomplished leaders in academic medicine and biomedical research, in conversation with &lt;strong&gt;Andrew J. Einstein, MD, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;, who will moderate the discussion. The informal format is designed to encourage open, candid conversation, with light refreshments provided.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The discussion will focus on career development in academic medicine, with particular emphasis on the experiences and needs of early clinical investigators navigating an increasingly complex and uncertain environment. This event is intended for assistant professors as well as junior and mid-career faculty seeking insight, mentorship, and community as they advance their academic careers.&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;TRANSFORM (TRaining And Nurturing Scholars&amp;nbsp;FOr Research that is Multidisciplinary) is the Irving Institute&amp;rsquo;s educational resource, administering the interdisciplinary master&amp;rsquo;s, predoctoral, postdoctoral, and career development programs.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TRANSFORM Evolution&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a community of current and alumni KL2 and Irving Scholars who support one another throughout the journey of academic medicine. The program hosts educational and social events throughout the year and fosters a network of scholars dedicated to both personal and professional growth. Evolution is co-chaired by Marisa Spann, PhD, Herbert Irving Associate Professor of Medical Psychology in Psychiatry; Badri N. Vardarajan, PhD, Assistant Professor of Neurological Science; and Bernard P. Chang, MD, PhD, Associate Dean of Faculty Health and Research Career Development.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Light refreshments will be served. Please register in advance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Embodied Learning: Object-Based Approaches to Inclusivity in the Classroom &lt;/strong&gt;(Part 1 of 2)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;How can pedagogical practices respond more fully to differences in access, ability, learning styles, and prior knowledge? This Learning Community invites participants from across disciplines to reflect on such questions by foregrounding the role of objects and the body in learning. We will reflect on how objects can transform pedagogical experiences, and how teaching with objects can cultivate more inclusive classrooms. The first session will address the practical side of our topic: how can teaching with objects enhance discussions within and beyond the classroom? How might instructors frame and prepare lessons centered around the study of objects, regardless of discipline? In the second session, we will address the theoretical dimension of embodied learning. This session will introduce and discuss key theoretical frameworks related to embodiment in cognitive approaches, learning through the body, object-based learning, object theory, and the pedagogies of artifacts. Participants will collectively assess how these theories challenge mind&amp;ndash;body dualisms operating in the classroom, reconceptualize knowledge as situated and material, and inform inclusive pedagogical practices that recognize learning as an embodied, relational, and multisensorial process.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This session is designed and led by Julia Carabatsos and Jazm&amp;iacute;n Novoa Lara, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the CTL.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: this is Part 1 of a two-part&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://ctl.columbia.edu/graduate-instructors/ctlgrads-learning-communities/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;. Participants are strongly encouraged to attend both sessions. Part 2 will convene Wednesday, February 11, 12:10-1:25pm in room 212 Butler Library. Register &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-9b69631a-019b-6a738293-00001591events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId=" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
